# Regression test: `timeit`'s timed closure runs on its own spawned thread (see
# time_run in src/builtins/control/timeit.rs), racing that thread's own completion
# against this job's control channel so pause/terminate are responsive even mid-run
# (not just between repeats). The timed closure's own *last statement* can itself be a
# stream-producing command (e.g. `files`, as opposed to a plain scalar value) --
# exactly the class of bug that's bitten other commands which hand a value off to
# another thread before fully consuming it (see tests/echo_background_stream_does_not_
# block.crush and commit 72cd57c4's own history of stream/thread-ownership races).
# `number`/`repeat` both > 1 so the timed closure actually runs more than once, the same
# reasoning tests/timeit_external_command_repeat.crush already documents for why a
# single run alone wouldn't have caught a repeat-specific regression.
$d := $(timeit number=2 repeat=2 it={files})
assert (($(typeof $d)) == $duration) "timeit should return a duration even when its timed closure's own output is a stream"
